diff --git a/scenes/gromen/narration/script.json b/scenes/gromen/narration/script.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1fc6072
--- /dev/null
+++ b/scenes/gromen/narration/script.json
@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+{
+  "title": "The Bondfire: Luke Gromen's Fiscal-Dominance Scenario",
+  "basis": "FFTT Tree Rings, October 2, 2026. A dramatization of the author's thesis, not a verified forecast or investment advice.",
+  "clips": [
+    {"id":"opening","title":"THE BONDFIRE","blocks":[
+      {"cue":"premise","text":"What if fighting inflation makes the government's debt problem worse?"},
+      {"cue":"system","text":"In Luke Gromen's scenario, Congress, retirees, banks, foreign investors, and geopolitical rivals all feed one combustible system."},
+      {"cue":"warning","text":"This is his October second thesis, dramatized. The explosion is a metaphor, not a forecast."}
+    ]},
+    {"id":"fiscal","title":"01 / THE PROMISES","blocks":[
+      {"cue":"retirees","text":"Start with aging boomers and other retirees. Social Security and health care create persistent spending commitments."},
+      {"cue":"congress","text":"Congress controls taxes and spending. Gromen assumes it cannot deliver enough politically acceptable adjustment."},
+      {"cue":"treasury","text":"Treasury, led in this scenario by Bessent, must finance the gap with bills and bonds."},
+      {"cue":"rollover","text":"As old debt matures, higher borrowing costs lift interest expense. Veterans' benefits add another long-lived commitment."}
+    ]},
+    {"id":"foreign","title":"02 / THE MISSING BUYERS","blocks":[
+      {"cue":"foreigners","text":"Foreign investors still buy American assets, but Gromen sees less reliable demand for Treasury bonds."},
+      {"cue":"china","text":"He interprets China's surplus recycling as shifting toward gold, commodities, and lending outside the United States."},
+      {"cue":"dollar","text":"A stronger dollar squeezes foreign dollar borrowers. They may sell American assets to obtain cash."},
+      {"cue":"buyers","text":"Domestic investors can absorb more debt, but potentially only at higher yields. Missing foreign flows do not literally mean no buyers."}
+    ]},
+    {"id":"supply","title":"03 / THE SUPPLY SQUEEZE","blocks":[
+      {"cue":"iran","text":"Iran and Hormuz disruptions keep oil expensive in Gromen's account. China's fuel export restrictions add pressure."},
+      {"cue":"copper","text":"Chile's copper disruptions constrain another essential input. Electrical infrastructure and the A I buildout need real materials."},
+      {"cue":"fed","text":"The Fed responds to inflation by keeping policy tight. Gromen fears higher yields will worsen fiscal stress."},
+      {"cue":"qualification","text":"Oil does not mechanically raise real yields. That link depends on inflation expectations and the Fed's response."}
+    ]},
+    {"id":"spiral","title":"04 / THE SELF-FEEDING LOOP","blocks":[
+      {"cue":"rates","text":"Higher yields raise refinancing costs."},
+      {"cue":"interest","text":"Higher interest expense widens deficits."},
+      {"cue":"deficit","text":"Bigger deficits require more issuance."},
+      {"cue":"issuance","text":"More issuance and reluctant buyers put pressure on yields again."},
+      {"cue":"growth","text":"Meanwhile, tighter conditions weaken growth and tax receipts. Gromen calls this a debt spiral."},
+      {"cue":"threshold","text":"He treats real rates above roughly two percent as dangerous. It is not an established universal crisis threshold."}
+    ]},
+    {"id":"ai","title":"05 / THE CREDIT ACCELERANT","blocks":[
+      {"cue":"hyperscalers","text":"A I hyperscalers borrow heavily for data centers, chips, and power. They compete for capital with other borrowers."},
+      {"cue":"workers","text":"In Gromen's adverse scenario, automation also displaces workers, weakening consumption and the tax base."},
+      {"cue":"credit","text":"Consumer delinquencies and corporate refinancing stress damage banks and private credit."},
+      {"cue":"collateral","text":"Banks and leveraged Treasury investors now face both funding pressure and falling collateral values. This is a scenario, not an inevitable A I outcome."}
+    ]},
+    {"id":"crisis","title":"06 / THE BREAKING POINT","blocks":[
+      {"cue":"riskoff","text":"First, the dollar rises while stocks, long bonds, and even gold or Bitcoin can sell off."},
+      {"cue":"move","text":"Gromen watches Treasury volatility, the MOVE index around one hundred twenty to one hundred thirty, for dysfunction."},
+      {"cue":"explosion","text":"Funding stress meets forced selling. The collateral engine breaks.","gapAfter":2.5},
+      {"cue":"choice","text":"The Fed faces his dramatic choice: tolerate deeper disruption, or intervene despite above-target inflation."}
+    ]},
+    {"id":"rescue","title":"07 / THE RELEASE VALVE","blocks":[
+      {"cue":"policy","text":"Gromen expects the Fed and Treasury to intervene. Bessent can alter issuance; the Fed can supply financing or purchase assets."},
+      {"cue":"reserves","text":"Fed lending or purchases expand its balance sheet and bank reserves. That is not automatically the same as expanding M two."},
+      {"cue":"m2","text":"M two includes public money balances. Bank credit and fiscal payments affect deposits; the final money growth depends on the operation."},
+      {"cue":"debase","text":"His crucial prediction is sustained accommodation of deficits: the dollar becomes the release valve, rather than bond yields."},
+      {"cue":"assets","text":"Gold, silver, and Bitcoin then outperform in his view. Stocks may rally in dollars while losing ground against gold."}
+    ]},
+    {"id":"dispute","title":"08 / WHERE THE STORY CAN CHANGE","blocks":[
+      {"cue":"gromen","text":"Gromen's strong point is political inertia. His leap is treating this particular ending as unavoidable."},
+      {"cue":"wang","text":"Joseph Wang distinguishes liquidity support from monetary easing. The Fed can repair funding markets while keeping policy rates high."},
+      {"cue":"mainstream","text":"Mainstream alternatives include phased tax increases, spending reform, and productivity growth. These are difficult, not mathematically impossible."},
+      {"cue":"ending","text":"Watch the primary deficit, effective borrowing costs, growth, inflation expectations, and the design of the rescue. Not just one yield or one money chart."}
+    ]}
