+<h2>Two distinctions that matter</h2><div class="distinctions"><article><h3>Reserves are not M2.</h3><p>The Fed's balance sheet records assets and liabilities. Bank reserves are Fed liabilities held by banks. M2 measures specified public money balances. Asset purchases, repo loans, fiscal payments and bank credit have different effects; there is no automatic one-to-one chain from reserves to M2 to inflation.</p></article><article><h3>A rescue is not one thing.</h3><p>Wang emphasizes that funding-market support can coexist with high policy rates. Gromen's inflationary ending requires an additional assumption: persistent fiscal accommodation that erodes real bond returns. Tax reform, spending reform, stronger productivity or a different intervention can alter the path.</p></article></div>
+<details id="transcript"><summary>The video script</summary><div class="script">__TRANSCRIPT__</div><p><a href="script.json">Machine-readable script</a> · <a href="captions.vtt">Caption file</a></p></details>
+<details><summary>Sources, scope and reproducibility</summary><div class="sources"><ul>
